WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:06.836
I do not suppose to know everything.

00:00:07.212 --> 00:00:12.717
And those closest to me, they might say,
I don't really know much at all, but --

00:00:12.759 --> 00:00:18.056
One thing that I know to be true
beyond the shadow of a doubt is this.

00:00:19.266 --> 00:00:23.770
The surest way to fail in life

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:25.146
is to set a goal.

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:28.984
(Laughter)

00:00:29.442 --> 00:00:32.404
Now, unfortunately, I found this out
through excruciating pain,

00:00:32.445 --> 00:00:35.782
but hopefully in the next nine minutes,
I can convince you, too,

00:00:35.824 --> 00:00:38.618
that this is true, without the pain.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:42.497
Unless, of course, you consider
listening to me painful.

00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:43.748
OK.

00:00:43.748 --> 00:00:45.709
The year was 2011.

00:00:46.293 --> 00:00:50.714
I was seeking to become
a multimillionaire by the age of 21.

00:00:51.256 --> 00:00:53.258
So how was I going to do this?

00:00:53.258 --> 00:00:54.551
It was simple.

00:00:54.593 --> 00:00:58.263
I was going to leave college
at the University of Texas

00:00:58.305 --> 00:00:59.514
one year early.

00:00:59.556 --> 00:01:01.600
I was going to leave after my junior year

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.311
and declare for the National
Football League draft.

00:01:04.686 --> 00:01:06.229
In order to do this, however,

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:09.107
you have to submit your resume to the NFL.

00:01:09.149 --> 00:01:10.942
A resume in football,

00:01:10.984 --> 00:01:15.530
it is your highlight tapes
from your years playing prior.

00:01:15.864 --> 00:01:18.617
So I submitted my football tape
from my freshman,

00:01:18.617 --> 00:01:21.036
my sophomore and my junior year.

00:01:21.745 --> 00:01:23.747
The NFL sent me back a letter.

00:01:24.164 --> 00:01:25.707
It simply read,

00:01:25.707 --> 00:01:27.083
"Dear Emmanuel,

00:01:27.125 --> 00:01:30.795
you will not be drafted
in the first three rounds."

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:33.882
Excuse you.

00:01:34.716 --> 00:01:36.843
I won't be drafted
in the first three rounds?

00:01:36.843 --> 00:01:38.345
So what did I do, TED audience?

00:01:38.386 --> 00:01:39.512
It's simple.

00:01:39.554 --> 00:01:41.431
I set a goal that day.

00:01:42.223 --> 00:01:44.392
But you know what they say
about goal-setting.

00:01:44.392 --> 00:01:47.020
You've got to write it down,
commit it to memory.

00:01:47.479 --> 00:01:49.230
So I highlighted the sheet of paper.

00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:53.151
“Dear Emmanuel, you won’t be drafted
in the first three rounds,”

00:01:53.193 --> 00:01:56.321
and I put it above my bed
in my condo in Austin, Texas.

00:01:56.363 --> 00:01:58.615
Every morning I woke up, I looked at it.

00:01:58.615 --> 00:02:01.660
"Dear Emmanuel, you won't be drafted
in the first three rounds."

00:02:01.701 --> 00:02:04.120
Every night before I went
to sleep, I looked at it.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:07.123
"Dear Emmanuel, you won't be drafted
in the first three rounds."

00:02:07.123 --> 00:02:11.503
I worked tirelessly
my senior year in college

00:02:11.544 --> 00:02:13.880
with one goal in mind.

00:02:14.464 --> 00:02:18.426
Be drafted in the first three
rounds of the NFL draft.

00:02:18.468 --> 00:02:21.137
Those are the most prestigious
rounds after all.

00:02:21.179 --> 00:02:24.391
6am workouts in college,
study hall, class.

00:02:24.432 --> 00:02:27.644
I'm doing it all, with one goal in mind.

00:02:28.478 --> 00:02:31.022
Well, fast forward one year later,

00:02:31.022 --> 00:02:32.691
I'm at the NFL Combine.

00:02:32.691 --> 00:02:35.944
The NFL Combine, it is the job interview.

00:02:36.277 --> 00:02:39.030
I'm in front of 32 billionaires.

00:02:39.072 --> 00:02:41.741
32 of the richest men in America,

00:02:41.783 --> 00:02:43.535
if not the richest men in the world.

00:02:43.576 --> 00:02:46.287
They are the owners of every NFL team.

00:02:47.664 --> 00:02:49.082
I'm running the 40-yard dash.

00:02:49.124 --> 00:02:53.795
The 40-yard dash is one of the testing
metrics you use at the NFL Combine

00:02:53.837 --> 00:02:55.630
with millions of dollars on the line.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:03:00.301
The faster you run,
the higher you get drafted.

00:03:01.386 --> 00:03:02.595
I take my mark.

00:03:03.513 --> 00:03:04.848
I proceed to run.

00:03:05.181 --> 00:03:07.851
I hear pop, pop, pop.

00:03:08.226 --> 00:03:09.394
I keep running.

00:03:09.436 --> 00:03:10.895
I think my heels are clicking.

00:03:10.895 --> 00:03:12.731
Pop, pop, pop.

00:03:13.356 --> 00:03:16.067
I clutch my quad, I fall to the ground.

00:03:16.067 --> 00:03:19.988
It was my quad being torn off the bone.

00:03:20.029 --> 00:03:21.197
(Audience: Oh!)

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.199
I ripped my quad off the bone,

00:03:23.199 --> 00:03:25.744
I’m falling [to the] ground in agony

00:03:25.785 --> 00:03:28.913
in front of these 32 billionaires.

00:03:30.165 --> 00:03:33.168
I didn't get drafted in the first
three rounds of the NFL draft.

00:03:34.252 --> 00:03:39.174
I made a decision that day,
in that moment,

00:03:39.215 --> 00:03:40.717
to stop setting goals.

00:03:40.759 --> 00:03:43.136
My self-esteem, ruined.

00:03:43.178 --> 00:03:45.138
Self-efficacy, ruined.

00:03:45.138 --> 00:03:48.558
That last year of my life
seemingly gone to waste

00:03:48.558 --> 00:03:50.310
because I had one goal, y'all.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:51.519
It was simple.

00:03:51.519 --> 00:03:53.313
Be drafted in the first three rounds.

00:03:53.313 --> 00:03:55.899
Then I tore my quad,
and it all fell apart.

00:03:58.359 --> 00:04:01.446
What I failed to mention thus far
is why I tore my quad.

00:04:02.655 --> 00:04:04.574
Two days prior at the NFL Combine,

00:04:04.616 --> 00:04:07.535
I weighed in at 240 pounds.

00:04:07.577 --> 00:04:10.038
In the NFL, the bigger
you are, the better.

00:04:10.038 --> 00:04:11.915
But as you all know, weight,

00:04:11.956 --> 00:04:13.875
it don't move all that fast.

00:04:13.875 --> 00:04:16.503
So I cut 10 pounds within two days.

00:04:16.503 --> 00:04:20.548
I ran the 40-yard dash
two days later at 230 pounds,

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:22.842
dehydrated, dieted for two days.

00:04:22.884 --> 00:04:27.764
Why? Because I was so focused on my goal

00:04:27.806 --> 00:04:29.682
that I caused harm to myself.

00:04:30.433 --> 00:04:32.602
It's not that goals don't add value.

00:04:32.936 --> 00:04:35.814
It’s just that the risk of goal-setting,

00:04:35.814 --> 00:04:37.941
it is greater than the potential reward.

00:04:37.941 --> 00:04:40.151
Think about it in your own
life for a moment.

00:04:40.485 --> 00:04:43.863
Maybe you had the goal
of being in a relationship.

00:04:44.197 --> 00:04:47.242
So you ignore the toxic behavior.

00:04:48.827 --> 00:04:50.912
Think about it in your own life.

00:04:50.954 --> 00:04:54.624
See, one of my greatest
accomplishments to date,

00:04:54.666 --> 00:04:55.834
being an NFL player,

00:04:55.834 --> 00:04:58.336
because ultimately
I did play for four years,

00:04:58.336 --> 00:05:00.129
I view negatively

00:05:00.129 --> 00:05:02.090
because I didn't hit my goal.

00:05:03.174 --> 00:05:05.134
Maybe that's the same for some of you.

00:05:06.052 --> 00:05:09.430
Maybe you have the goal of reading
a certain amount of books in a year.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:13.101
You've become a much more proficient
reader, but you didn't hit your goal.

00:05:13.101 --> 00:05:17.021
Maybe you had a goal of making a certain
amount of money for your business.

00:05:17.021 --> 00:05:22.610
You are a thriving entrepreneur,
but you didn't hit your goal.

00:05:23.486 --> 00:05:24.696
I left the NFL.

00:05:25.154 --> 00:05:26.948
I became a content creator

00:05:26.948 --> 00:05:29.492
where I would live
a much more limitless life.

00:05:29.993 --> 00:05:32.078
I'll get to that more here in a moment.

00:05:32.412 --> 00:05:35.790
The question now is why
in the world do we set goals?

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:38.501
Well, first, let's define a goal.

00:05:39.377 --> 00:05:42.213
A goal, by definition, it is the end

00:05:42.213 --> 00:05:44.299
towards which effort is aimed.

00:05:45.091 --> 00:05:49.095
But why would I start something
with the end in mind?

00:05:49.512 --> 00:05:52.098
In working on my master’s degree
in sports psychology,

00:05:52.098 --> 00:05:54.809
I focused on goal-setting,
I wrote my final thesis on it,

00:05:54.809 --> 00:05:58.146
and I realized we set goals
for three primary reasons.

00:05:58.187 --> 00:05:59.314
The first.

00:06:00.732 --> 00:06:05.445
We've been indoctrinated with this belief
that we have to set a goal to achieve.

00:06:05.445 --> 00:06:08.823
There's a popularized study,
which has since been debunked,

00:06:08.865 --> 00:06:11.159
which said the person
who writes their goals down

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:12.493
and commits them to memory,

00:06:12.535 --> 00:06:15.496
they will earn twice as much money
as the person who doesn't.

00:06:16.372 --> 00:06:18.791
So what you want me to believe

00:06:18.791 --> 00:06:21.502
is the difference
between one million dollars

00:06:21.544 --> 00:06:24.005
and 500,000 dollars is this?

00:06:25.715 --> 00:06:27.091
What you want me to believe

00:06:27.133 --> 00:06:31.679
is the difference between 100,000 dollars
and 50,000 dollars is simply this?

00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:35.516
Can't be.

00:06:35.558 --> 00:06:38.186
Now, these ideas
aren't completely off-base.

00:06:38.227 --> 00:06:39.979
The problem is very simple.

00:06:39.979 --> 00:06:43.024
They're misidentifying
causation and correlation.

00:06:43.024 --> 00:06:44.776
Surely there is a correlation

00:06:44.817 --> 00:06:47.403
between the person who was willing
to go the extra mile

00:06:47.403 --> 00:06:49.072
and write their goal down

00:06:49.113 --> 00:06:52.325
and the person who's willing to go
the extra mile in their business.

00:06:52.325 --> 00:06:54.786
But no, the reason you're making
twice as much money

00:06:54.827 --> 00:06:56.788
is not because you wrote your goal down.

00:06:56.788 --> 00:06:58.998
The second reason we set goals,

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:01.167
goals give us a North Star.

00:07:01.167 --> 00:07:02.502
Motivation.

00:07:02.543 --> 00:07:05.713
As if we'd just be wandering
around life aimlessly

00:07:05.713 --> 00:07:07.256
if we didn't set a goal.

00:07:07.674 --> 00:07:09.634
The final reason we set goals?

00:07:10.426 --> 00:07:13.346
We have this insatiable
desire for feedback.

00:07:13.346 --> 00:07:14.722
And feedback,

00:07:14.722 --> 00:07:18.226
it is a predecessor, is a necessary
aspect of goal-setting.

00:07:18.267 --> 00:07:19.811
Think about it in your own life.

00:07:19.811 --> 00:07:24.065
Did this Instagram post get as many
likes as that Instagram post?

00:07:24.107 --> 00:07:27.026
Did this tweet get as many likes
as my last tweet?

00:07:27.026 --> 00:07:29.153
How about this TikTok post?

00:07:29.195 --> 00:07:30.989
Did it get as many shares as my last?

00:07:30.989 --> 00:07:33.866
We have this insatiable
desire for feedback.

00:07:35.076 --> 00:07:36.786
You know, I made a mistake earlier.

00:07:37.245 --> 00:07:39.872
I said the surest way
to fail in life is to set a goal.

00:07:40.707 --> 00:07:43.084
But in all honesty,

00:07:43.084 --> 00:07:46.004
the only way to fail in life
is to set a goal.

00:07:46.004 --> 00:07:48.047
Think about anything
you've ever failed at.

00:07:48.089 --> 00:07:51.300
Maybe relationally,
occupationally, educationally.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.344
That marriage, it ended in divorce.

00:07:53.344 --> 00:07:55.805
You didn't get into that college
that you wanted to.

00:07:55.847 --> 00:07:58.266
You didn't pass the MCAT,
you didn't pass the LSAT,

00:07:58.266 --> 00:07:59.475
whatever the case may be,

00:07:59.475 --> 00:08:01.602
it was all tethered to a goal.

00:08:01.936 --> 00:08:05.106
It's not just that the surest way
to fail in life is to set a goal.

00:08:05.106 --> 00:08:07.275
The only way to fail in life
is to set a goal.

00:08:07.316 --> 00:08:09.277
So what do I suggest we do instead?

00:08:09.318 --> 00:08:10.445
Here it is.

00:08:11.404 --> 00:08:14.574
You have an objective with no limitations.

00:08:14.615 --> 00:08:17.243
Now I know what you're thinking,
Acho, that's semantics.

00:08:17.243 --> 00:08:20.913
No, this small difference will have
a huge impact in your life.

00:08:20.955 --> 00:08:23.541
Objective: effort aimed in a direction.

00:08:23.875 --> 00:08:27.712
A goal: the end towards which
energy is aimed.

00:08:29.589 --> 00:08:31.632
Your objective, it should be subjective.

00:08:31.674 --> 00:08:33.968
It should be subject
to people's interpretation.

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:36.512
That way you do not fail.

00:08:38.097 --> 00:08:40.808
The theme of this conference
is Possibilities.

00:08:41.642 --> 00:08:45.229
You all have spent money,
left your homes, maybe children,

00:08:45.271 --> 00:08:47.482
because somewhere deep down inside you,

00:08:47.482 --> 00:08:49.942
you subscribe to this notion
of possibility.

00:08:51.402 --> 00:08:55.239
But you can't simultaneously subscribe
to the notion of possibility

00:08:55.281 --> 00:08:57.909
while still being held captive
by goal-setting.

00:08:58.409 --> 00:09:04.290
The laws of goal-setting completely
undermine the freedom of possibility.

00:09:04.916 --> 00:09:07.877
So if we're being honest with ourselves,
we have to choose one.

00:09:07.919 --> 00:09:10.922
Because possibility is limitless.

00:09:11.339 --> 00:09:14.217
But goal-setting, oh,
it’s fixated on an end.

00:09:15.259 --> 00:09:17.553
If you want to have
a 10-million dollar business,

00:09:17.595 --> 00:09:18.721
you'll set that goal.

00:09:18.763 --> 00:09:20.765
And you might do just that.

00:09:20.807 --> 00:09:22.433
Have a 10-million dollar business.

00:09:22.475 --> 00:09:23.684
Congratulations.

00:09:23.726 --> 00:09:26.020
You'll work towards
a 10-million dollar business.

00:09:26.062 --> 00:09:27.814
But what if it could have been more?

00:09:27.814 --> 00:09:30.441
If you want to run a mile
without stopping,

00:09:30.483 --> 00:09:33.611
you’ll work towards it,
and you might do just that.

00:09:33.945 --> 00:09:36.697
Having never known
if you could have run a marathon.

00:09:38.116 --> 00:09:40.201
What does it look like practically?

00:09:41.035 --> 00:09:44.413
Kirk Hammett, lead guitarist
for Metallica,

00:09:44.455 --> 00:09:47.792
he never had the goal
of winning eight Grammys.

00:09:48.084 --> 00:09:52.380
He never had the goal
of selling 125 million records,

00:09:52.421 --> 00:09:54.632
never had the goal of being a rock star.

00:09:54.632 --> 00:09:57.218
He simply said, and I quote,

00:09:57.218 --> 00:10:00.346
"I just want to play my guitar
a little bit better every day."

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:02.265
Close quote.

00:10:02.265 --> 00:10:04.976
See, because with that, there is no end.

00:10:05.476 --> 00:10:08.688
A goal, by its definition,
it is focused on an ending,

00:10:08.729 --> 00:10:11.774
which means inherently a goal is limiting.

00:10:11.816 --> 00:10:14.735
It's not that goals in
and of themselves have no value,

00:10:14.735 --> 00:10:17.780
it's just, what about the damage
you have done to yourself,

00:10:17.822 --> 00:10:20.283
your self-esteem, your self-efficacy,

00:10:20.283 --> 00:10:23.161
the way in which you view yourself
when you look at yourself?

00:10:23.202 --> 00:10:24.662
What about that damage?

00:10:25.371 --> 00:10:27.248
I'm not saying don't achieve.

00:10:27.248 --> 00:10:29.709
I'm saying achieve limitlessly.

00:10:29.709 --> 00:10:32.920
I'm saying believe in the reason
you were all sitting in here today

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:34.630
because you believe in possibility.

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:36.924
Let go of the captivity of goal-setting

00:10:36.924 --> 00:10:40.428
and achieve all
you actually have to achieve.

00:10:41.345 --> 00:10:44.515
So what is my objective
with no limitations?

00:10:45.224 --> 00:10:46.350
Very simply put,

00:10:46.392 --> 00:10:48.895
to be considered one
of the most creative people

00:10:48.895 --> 00:10:51.439
the television industry has ever seen.

00:10:52.064 --> 00:10:55.109
Because if I would have achieved my goal

00:10:55.109 --> 00:10:59.030
of being drafted in the first
three rounds of the NFL draft,

00:10:59.071 --> 00:11:00.907
I never would have achieved this.

00:11:02.116 --> 00:11:05.411
Winning a Primetime Emmy Award
and sharing that moment with my mom.

00:11:06.704 --> 00:11:12.835
(Applause)

00:11:13.169 --> 00:11:15.546
But just as importantly,

00:11:15.546 --> 00:11:17.089
if I would have achieved my goal

00:11:17.131 --> 00:11:19.967
of being drafted in the first
three rounds of the NFL draft,

00:11:20.009 --> 00:11:22.511
I wouldn't be standing
in front of you all today.

00:11:22.511 --> 00:11:23.679
Why?

00:11:24.263 --> 00:11:26.849
Because if you hear nothing else,

00:11:26.891 --> 00:11:29.560
truly, if you hear nothing else,

00:11:29.602 --> 00:11:30.895
hear this.

00:11:30.895 --> 00:11:33.439
I'm begging each and every one
of you to hear this.

00:11:33.898 --> 00:11:37.151
The goal that you achieve,

00:11:37.193 --> 00:11:41.072
it's actually the penalty that you receive

00:11:41.113 --> 00:11:44.408
for having set that goal
in the first place.

00:11:44.408 --> 00:11:45.576
Thank you.

00:11:45.618 --> 00:11:52.500
(Applause and cheers)

